# Scheduling ElixirConf

[![Run in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/badge/v1/blue.svg)](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Felixir-tempo%2Fagenda%2Fmain%2Flivebook%2Felixir_melbourne_september_2026.livemd)

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:agenda, github: "elixir-tempo/agenda"}
])
```

## Agenda

<!-- slide: logo https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kipcole9/tempo/refs/heads/main/assets/logo.png -->

<!-- slide: author Kip Cole -->

<!-- slide: byline Author of Tempo, Localize, Elixir Unicode, Image, Text, ... -->

<!-- slide: event Melbourne Elixir Meetup -->

<!-- slide: date 3 September 2026 -->

Everything in this talk runs. The data is the real ElixirConf US 2025 programme, taken from the published schedule, and every number on these slides came out of the notebook rather than out of my head.

* Tempo answers *when is this free?*
* Agenda answers *what should I book, and where?*
* Built on Tempo, which treats every date and time as an interval
* Today: the real ElixirConf US 2025 programme, laid out from scratch

## What we keep rebuilding

Every scheduling system I have worked on rebuilds the same three things above the calendar, and none of them is the interesting part of the product.

* A map of resources, and hand-rolled filters over its keys
* A travel matrix, hard-coded, usually wrong
* Free/busy state stored somewhere, and slowly going stale

The third one is the killer. Once availability is *stored* rather than *derived*, every code path that books something has to remember to release it, and one that forgets leaves a room permanently missing.

## The five nouns

Learn these and the rest of the library follows.

* **Resource** — a named thing that can be allocated: a room, a person, a projector
* **Place** — a tree of containers, so travel time is *derived* rather than configured
* **Session** — what is being scheduled: how long, inside what window, needing what
* **Arrangement** — one candidate way to hold it: a time and a resource per role
* **Allocation** — one resource bound to one session; the **ledger** is the set of them

## Words we keep apart

The nouns are easy. The vocabulary is the part that carries the design, because each of these pairs is one word in most schedulers and two here — and every one of them is a bug when they get merged.

* **Concurrency** is how many claims may overlap at an *instant*. A **limit** is how much falls inside a *stretch of calendar*, however far apart. A 200-seat hall is `concurrency: 1`; a nurse's contract is `limits: [week: 5]`.
* **`needs`** describes and lets the planner choose. **`roster`** names and accepts no substitute. A room is usually described; a speaker is always named.
* **Open** is when a resource could be used. **Busy** is what already holds it. **Free** is `open − busy`, derived on every call — never stored, so it cannot go stale.
* An **arrangement** is a *proposal*: one time, one resource per role. An **allocation** is the *record* of one resource actually bound. The ledger holds allocations; the search returns arrangements.
* **Claim** books and refuses what it cannot honour. **Record** writes down what happened and checks nothing. A schedule is a request; a timesheet is a fact.
* A **place** contains; an **attribute** merely labels. `location: :sydney` answers *"is it in Sydney?"*; a place tree answers *"can a delegate get there in the break?"* — and says `{:error, :unknown}` rather than guessing.
* A **session** is one thing to be held. A **track** is a family of sessions constrained against *each other* — a shared audience, so no two may run at once.

## Words we refused

Every one of these was the obvious first choice, and each is written down in the moduledoc that rejected it.

* **Session**, not *meeting* (a court hearing and a conference talk are neither), not *event* (`Tempo.Event` is taken, and it collides with event sourcing), not *booking* (which names the request in AshScheduling, not the thing requested).
* **Programme**, not *schedule* — triple-booked already: `Tempo.Schedule` is critical-path planning, `AshScheduling.Schedule` is a bookable resource, and colloquially it is this output.
* **Track**, not *stream* — Elixir's `Stream` owns that name and a conference stream would shadow it at every call site.

Naming is the cheapest constraint in the library. Every pair on the previous slide would compile perfectly well as a single word, and each would take a real distinction with it.

## The conference

Two days at the Renaissance Orlando at SeaWorld, three parallel rooms, and a fourth room used for registration, coffee, lunch and the reception rather than for talks.

| | |
| --- | --- |
| Days | Thursday 28 – Friday 29 August 2025 |
| Talk rooms | Peninsula 4-7, Canaveral, Biscayne |
| Breakout talks | 42 |
| Plenaries | 4 keynotes and updates |
| Session lengths | 20 and 40 minutes |
| Speakers | 45 |

## Breaks are not constraints

The first thing to model is when the rooms are *open*, and it is not a range — talks run 10:45 to 12:45, break for lunch, resume at 13:45, break again for coffee.

Lunch is not a rule to enforce. It is simply time the rooms are not open, and every calculation downstream inherits that for free.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
import Agenda.Predicate
import Tempo.Sigils

alias Agenda.Arrangement

{:ok, talk_hours} =
  Tempo.IntervalSet.new([
    ~o"2025-08-28T10:45:00/2025-08-28T12:45:00",
    ~o"2025-08-28T13:45:00/2025-08-28T15:05:00",
    ~o"2025-08-28T15:35:00/2025-08-28T16:05:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T10:40:00/2025-08-29T12:40:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T13:40:00/2025-08-29T15:20:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T15:50:00/2025-08-29T16:20:00"
  ])

Tempo.IntervalSet.count(talk_hours)
```

## The rooms

Peninsula 4-7 is also the plenary room, so it opens earlier and closes later than the other two.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
venue = Agenda.place("Renaissance Orlando at SeaWorld")

{:ok, plenary_hours} =
  Tempo.IntervalSet.new([
    ~o"2025-08-28T09:00:00/2025-08-28T10:15:00",
    ~o"2025-08-28T10:45:00/2025-08-28T12:45:00",
    ~o"2025-08-28T13:45:00/2025-08-28T15:05:00",
    ~o"2025-08-28T15:35:00/2025-08-28T17:50:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T09:00:00/2025-08-29T10:10:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T10:40:00/2025-08-29T12:40:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T13:40:00/2025-08-29T15:20:00",
    ~o"2025-08-29T15:50:00/2025-08-29T17:30:00"
  ])

room = fn name, seats, hours ->
  {:ok, resource} = Agenda.resource(name, within: venue, seats: seats) |> Agenda.open(hours)
  resource
end

peninsula = room.("Peninsula 4-7", 700, plenary_hours)
canaveral = room.("Canaveral", 250, talk_hours)
biscayne = room.("Biscayne", 250, talk_hours)

rooms = [peninsula, canaveral, biscayne]
Enum.map(rooms, & &1.name)
```

## Speakers are resources

This is the one idea in the talk. Not an attribute of a talk, not a string in a metadata map — a resource, exactly like a room, with the same default concurrency of one.

* A room holds one session at a time. So does a person.
* Nothing else has to change: same struct, same availability, same clash rule
* Rooms are chosen by description; people are named
* This is what invented demo data never has

## Why it matters here

I did not plan this example around the constraint. I loaded the real programme and the constraint was already in it.

* Allison Randal keynoted Thursday 09:15 — and joined the 10:45 panel
* Anna Sherman was on that panel, and spoke again on Friday
* Four people shared the panel between them
* A scheduler treating a speaker as a label cannot see any of this

## Describing a talk

`needs/3` describes and lets the planner choose; `roster/3` names and accepts no substitutes. Both bind the same way once the search starts.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
speaker_names =
  ~w(Hugo|Baraúna Misael|Perez_Chamorro Savannah|Manning Anna|Sherman Allison|Randal
     Lorena|Mireles Chris|Nelson Aaron|Votre Carlos|Souza Emmanuel|Pinault Jordan|Miller
     Britton|Broderick Dennis|Beatty Nicholas|Henry Yuri|Oliveira Stephanie|Lane
     Joe|Harrow Juan|Azambuja Krzysztof|Nalepa Noah|Betzen Nathan|Hessler
     Mitchell|Hanberg Jacob|Swanner Adam|Kirk George|Guimarães Jinkyou|Son Matt|Ludwigs
     Tyler|Young Jonatan|Männchen John|Darrington Christian|Koch Jeremy|Searls
     Igor|Spiridonov Gonzalo|Rodriguez Michael|St_Clair Micah|Woods Ivy|Markwell
     Kate|Rezentes Paulo|Valente Daniel|Kentfield Dan|Janowski Brett|Beatty
     German|Velasco Doug|VonMoser Geoffrey|Lessel)
  |> Enum.map(&(&1 |> String.replace("|", " ") |> String.replace("_", " ")))

speakers =
  Enum.map(speaker_names, fn name ->
    {:ok, speaker} = Agenda.resource(name, role: :speaker) |> Agenda.open(plenary_hours)
    speaker
  end)

by_name = Map.new(speakers, &{&1.name, &1})
length(speakers)
```

## One roster call per role

A trap worth knowing, because it silently costs you the exact constraint this talk is about.

* `roster(:speaker, [a, b, c])` binds all three
* `roster(:speaker, [a])` three times keeps only the last
* No error, no warning — the panel simply loses three of its four people
* I lost twenty minutes to this while building the example

## The programme

Forty-two talks, each naming its duration, its day, the kind of room it needs and the people who must be there.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
talks = [
  {"The Architecture Behind Deploying Livebook Apps", 40, :thu, ["Hugo Baraúna"]},
  {"From Complexity to Clarity", 40, :thu, ["Misael Perez Chamorro"]},
  {"Building Careers, Balancing Life", 40, :thu,
